            <ArticleTitle>Multiple beam ptychography for large field-of-view, high throughput, quantitative phase contrast imaging.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>164-171</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0304-3991(16)30301-1</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ultramic.2017.08.018</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The ability to record large field-of-view images without a loss in spatial resolution is of crucial importance for imaging science. For most imaging techniques however, an increase in field-of-view comes at the cost of decreased resolution. Here we present a novel extension to ptychographic coherent diffractive imaging that permits simultaneous full-field imaging of multiple locations by illuminating the sample with spatially separated, interfering probes. This technique allows for large field-of-view imaging in amplitude and phase while maintaining diffraction-limited resolution, without an increase in collected data i.e. diffraction patterns acquired.</AbstractText>
